  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\st0156_0e992749cf56.png\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 181px;\" /></p><p>Aromatic&nbsp;compounds are cyclic, planar and must obey Huckel&rsquo;s&nbsp;rule i.e. molecule must have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math> pi-electrons where n is an integer (i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#8230;</mo></math>etc.).&nbsp; The compound B follws the Huckel&#39;s rule. The acid-base reactions fast reactions. Compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>)</mo></math>, also called squaric acid is a strong acid. So, above reaction proceeds very fast. All <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> bond lengths in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;are same due to resonance. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;shows tautomerism. A</p>"
  }
